- Self-reference: `if (parent.id === child.id) return false;` — covers solutions too. ✓

But wait — I should double check the custom node edge creation more carefully. Let me re-read `canCreateEdge`:

```js
export const canCreateEdge = (topicGraph: Graph, parent: Node, child: Node) => {
  const relation = getRelation(parent.type, child.type);
  const existingEdge = topicGraph.edges.find(...);
  if (parent.id === child.id) { ... return false; }
  if (existingEdge) { ... return false; }
  const secondParentForClaim = claimNodeTypes.includes(child.type) && ...;
  ...
  if (!relation) { ... return false; }
  return true;
};
```

For custom nodes, relation is always the relatesTo fallback, so canCreateEdge returns true (assuming not self/dup). So drag-drop edge creation to/from custom nodes is supported. ✓ No change needed.

Now, one thing I want to verify more carefully: the requirement says "creating edges from custom nodes is supported". Let me verify there isn't a place where custom nodes are excluded from being edge sources. Let me check the reactflow setup — the `nodesConnectable` prop is `userCanEditTopicData` (boolean), applies to all nodes including custom. And each node gets a NodeHandle (source/target handles) for parent/child directions. Custom nodes get these handles too. So connections from custom nodes are supported in the UI.

Let me look at NodeHandle styles to verify handles are rendered for all node types (they should be, given FlowNode renders NodeHandle for all nodes unconditionally). Yes: `FlowNode` renders `<NodeHandle node={node} direction="parent" .../>` and `<NodeHandle node={node} direction="child" .../>` unconditionally. ✓

Now, self-referencing solution edges: The `canCreateEdge` guard handles the drag path. But let me reconsider whether the handoff wants an additional explicit guard in `createEdgeAndImpliedEdges`/`addNode`. Since `addNode` always creates a brand-new node before connecting, a self-edge can't occur via addNode. I'm confident the existing generic guard fully satisfies the requirement.

Hmm, but wait — let me reconsider once more. There might be a subtle issue with `getConnectingEdge`:

```js
export const getConnectingEdge = (graphPartId1: string, graphPartId2: string, edges: Edge[]) => {
  const edge = edges.find(
    (edge) =>
      (edge.source === graphPartId1 && edge.target === graphPartId2) ||
      (edge.source === graphPartId2 && edge.target === graphPartId2)   // <-- bug: compares target to graphPartId2 in both
  );
  return edge;
};
```

This is used by `createEdgeAndImpliedEdges` to avoid duplicate edges. For addNode with accomplishes: new node id is fresh, so no existing edge. Fine. This bug is pre-existing and not in scope.

